      ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+112}$ at the point where $x=4.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2{x}^{2}+112.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+112}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+112}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+112}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(4)}{2\\sqrt{2(4{)}^{2}+112}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2\\sqrt{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
