      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-3{x}^{2}+91.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-6x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x}{2\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+91}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+91}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(-\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+91}})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6(3)}{2\\sqrt{-3(3{)}^{2}+91}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18}{2\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
